# Inkling 2 Small on purpose-built silicon

This is a hardware-architecture study by Aercrist. The study is about a chip built around
[Inkling 2 Small](https://huggingface.co/thinkingmachines/Inkling-2-Small) (594B parameters, 17B
active per token). It asks four questions:

- What does this chip deliver against the best published GPU deployment?
- Which memory architecture gets that result?
- What is the cost to keep the weights reloadable?
- How does the answer change for smaller models?

This study is the sequel to our [DeepSeek-V4.1 study](https://github.com/Aercrist/dsv41-accel-study)
and follows its rules:

- We render every number from `results/*.json`.
- Every constant has a source or a sweep.
- We wrote the predictions before we simulated anything.

This study is independent. It is not affiliated with or endorsed by Thinking Machines Lab.

**We fabricated nothing, and we measured nothing on hardware.** The GPU figures reproduce the published
measurements of other people. The chips are designs on paper. We synthesized one circuit block.

## Findings

The table compares a chip that keeps every weight in reloadable on-die memory with B300 GPUs. The
GPUs run the best engines published today, in the best layout for each metric (2x, 4x or 8x B300).
The median and the range are from {{ MC.n }} joint draws of
{{ U.params | length }} uncertain inputs.

| | median | 5th to 95th percentile | central case | confidence |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **speed for one user** | **{{ x(MC.speed_batch1.median) }}** | {{ x(MC.speed_batch1.p05) }} to {{ x(MC.speed_batch1.p95) }} | {{ x(RS.speed_batch1) }} ({{ n(s.batch1.tok_s_user, -2) }} against {{ n(g4.batch1.tok_s_user, -1) }} tok/s) | medium: the GPU side is calibrated; the chip side takes its time per operation from a published machine |
| **tokens per joule**, decode, at each platform's best interactive point | **{{ x(MC.tokens_per_joule_best.median) }}** | {{ x(MC.tokens_per_joule_best.p05) }} to {{ x(MC.tokens_per_joule_best.p95) }} | {{ x(RS.tokens_per_joule_best) }} | medium |
| **cost per token**, decode, at the same point | **{{ x(MC.cost_best.median, 2) }}** | {{ x(MC.cost_best.p05, 2) }} to {{ x(MC.cost_best.p95, 2) }} | {{ x(RS.cost_best) }} | low: rests on token demand, lifetime and margin |
| **cost of reloadable weights** against fixed weights in the masks | | | {{ x(I.designs.s_over_f.dies) }} the silicon; speed not separable: the two designs' speeds come from two different published machines | medium on silicon: the fixed-weight side scales one shipped 8B chip |

1. **On-chip weights give speed and energy gains, not cheap tokens.** One user gets about
   {{ n(s.batch1.tok_s_user, -2) }} tokens a second instead of {{ n(g4.batch1.tok_s_user, -1) }}. A busy system delivers {{ x(RS.tokens_per_joule_best) }} the
   tokens per joule. At {{ n(I.headline.sla_tok_s_user) }} tok/s per user, the decode cost of the chip is less than 2x above or below the GPU decode cost (median {{ x(MC.cost_best.median, 2) }}). At
   {{ n(I.thresholds.rows[-1].tok_s_user_min, 0) }} tok/s per user and above, the GPUs hold only a few users each, and the chip is
   {{ x(I.thresholds.rows[-1].s.cost, 0) }} cheaper. Our GPU model is least certain at these batch sizes.
2. **The memory architecture makes the difference, and software comes first.** Better software on the same
   GPUs gives {{ x(RG.speed_batch1) }} for a single user. A purpose-built chip that keeps its weights in HBM
   adds no speed to that gain ({{ x(R["a_x4/g1_x4"].speed_batch1, 2) }}). But it is the cheapest whole system. Its cost is {{ x(I.end_to_end.rows.a_x4.cost_gain) }} lower
   than the cost of GPUs when we count prefill.
3. **The chip can do its own prefill if it pipelines the prompts.** On the published agentic workload, prefill is
   {{ pct(I.end_to_end.rows.g0.prefill_share_of_cost) }} of the GPU cost per output token. With {{ I.prefill.rows.s.prompts_in_flight }} prompts in flight, the on-chip design
   prefills at {{ x(I.prefill.s_over_g0_cost, 2) }} the cost per token of the GPUs. End to end, it is {{ x(I.end_to_end.rows.s.cost_gain) }} lower in cost and {{ x(I.end_to_end.rows.s.tokens_per_joule_gain) }}
   better in tokens per joule than GPUs. With HBM chips for prefill, the gains are {{ x(I.end_to_end.rows.s_decode_a_prefill.cost_gain) }} and {{ x(I.end_to_end.rows.s_decode_a_prefill.tokens_per_joule_gain) }}.
4. **Reloadable weights cost {{ x(I.designs.s_over_f.dies) }} the silicon.** In return, there are three die designs instead of
   {{ I.designs.f.cost.distinct_die_designs }}. A new model version needs a load of weights, not {{ usd(I.designs.f.cost.unique_mask_usd / 1e6, 0) }}M of new masks. The
   synthesized block shows that a fixed weight in a multiplier saves only {{ x(SS.reloadable_over_fixed.hardwired_lane_area, 2) }} in the area of that lane.

   The silicon
   difference is SRAM against mask-programmed storage, plus the multiplier lanes of S ({{ pct(I.designs.s.area.mac_lanes / I.designs.s.area.silicon_mm2) }} of its silicon).
   The density of the fixed design comes from one shipped chip that has its arithmetic folded in. The higher speed of the fixed design in our tables is not separable. The speeds of the two designs come from two different published machines, not from a property of reloadability.
5. **The chip needs scale.** A system has {{ I.designs.s.area.dies }} dies and costs about {{ usd(I.designs.s.cost.total / 1e6, 1) }}M with its share of design cost.
   It breaks even with GPUs at about {{ n(I.demand.breakeven_tokens_per_day.s / 1e12, 1) }} trillion tokens a day of this one model.
6. **The gain becomes smaller down the ladder for models with the shape of this one.** We predicted the opposite.
   At about 8B parameters the speed gain is {{ x(I.ladder.rung_8b.gain.S.speed_batch1) }}. For dense models, the published machines show
   that the gain becomes larger.

Our prior was a gain of about 2-10x. The speed gain and the energy gain are in that range. The cost gain is below it.
Two adversarial reviews changed these numbers by a large amount ([05](docs/05_limitations.md)). Before
the reviews, the cost figure was {{ x(RV.headline_change["s/g0:cost_best"].before) if RV else "much higher" }}.

